# Posing with Poserx

```elixir
poserx_path = Path.expand("~/src/elixir/poserx")

Mix.install([{:ex_poser, path: poserx_path}])
```

## Before you start

This notebook drives a live Poser 14: Poser must be running with the Poser
Bridge add-on deployed (see [Setup](setup.md)) and a scene with at least one
figure loaded. Set `poserx_path` above to your checkout of the repo.

Every cell that changes the scene puts it back by the end, so the scene is
left as you found it.

## Connect

```elixir
{:ok, _} = ExPoser.Client.start_link()
```

The handshake runs in the background, so wait for it before the first call.
`ExPoser.Client.await_connected/1` returns `:ok` once the client is connected,
or a `:timeout` error after five seconds.

```elixir
:ok = ExPoser.Client.await_connected()
ExPoser.Client.info()
```

## The scene

Frames count from 0 here and from 1 in the GUI: `frame: 0` is GUI frame 1.

```elixir
%{
  frame: ExPoser.Scene.frame!(),
  num_frames: ExPoser.Scene.num_frames!(),
  fps: ExPoser.Scene.fps!(),
  figures: ExPoser.Scene.figures!()
}
```

Pick a figure. The inspect output shows its internal name; `name` is the GUI
label.

```elixir
fig = ExPoser.Scene.figures!() |> hd()
{fig.internal, fig.name}
```

## Body parts and dials

A listing takes two requests however long it is.

```elixir
actors = ExPoser.Figure.actors!(fig)
Enum.map(actors, &{&1.internal, &1.name})
```

Pick a body part by internal or display name. Change `actor_name` to one from
the list above.

```elixir
actor_name = "BODY:1"
actor = ExPoser.Figure.actor!(fig, actor_name)
```

Every dial on it, with its value, in two requests:

```elixir
ExPoser.Actor.values!(actor)
```

Pick a dial. The idiomatic structs are plain data: they name the dial and
hold nothing live.

```elixir
parm_name = "yrot"
parm = ExPoser.Actor.parameter!(actor, parm_name)
{parm, ExPoser.Ref.path(parm), ExPoser.Parameter.min_max!(parm)}
```

## Set a dial at a frame

Set the dial at `frame: 4` (GUI frame 5) and read it back at a few frames.
Poser keys the value at frame 4, and every dial already has a key at frame 0
(GUI frame 1). The current frame doesn't move.

```elixir
before = ExPoser.Parameter.value!(parm, frame: 4)
keys_before = ExPoser.Parameter.keyframes!(parm)
start_frame = ExPoser.Scene.frame!()

try do
  ExPoser.Parameter.set_value!(parm, 30.0, frame: 4)

  %{
    keyframes: ExPoser.Parameter.keyframes!(parm),
    at_0: ExPoser.Parameter.value_at!(parm, 0),
    at_2: ExPoser.Parameter.value_at!(parm, 2),
    at_4: ExPoser.Parameter.value_at!(parm, 4),
    frame_unchanged?: ExPoser.Scene.frame!() == start_frame
  }
after
  # Put the scene back, even if a read raised: the old value, and no key at
  # frame 4 unless one was there.
  ExPoser.Parameter.set_value!(parm, before, frame: 4)
  if 4 not in keys_before, do: ExPoser.Parameter.delete_keyframe!(parm, 4)
end
```

## A batch

One request: three reads at three frames. Results come back in op order.

```elixir
ExPoser.batch!(fn b ->
  b
  |> ExPoser.Batch.get(parm, frame: 0)
  |> ExPoser.Batch.get(parm, frame: 10)
  |> ExPoser.Batch.get(parm, frame: 20)
end)
```

Errors are values. A missing dial gives `:not_found` with the path and the
index of the op that failed:

```elixir
missing = %ExPoser.Parameter{actor: actor, internal: "no_such_dial", name: "no_such_dial"}

ExPoser.batch(fn b ->
  b
  |> ExPoser.Batch.get(parm)
  |> ExPoser.Batch.get(missing)
end)
```

## Down to ExPoser.Api

`handle/2` returns a live handle for the generated `ExPoser.Api` modules. It is
yours to release.

```elixir
h = ExPoser.Parameter.handle!(parm)

try do
  {:ok, internal} = ExPoser.Api.ParmType.internal_name(h)
  {:ok, hidden} = ExPoser.Api.ParmType.hidden(h)
  %{internal_name: internal, hidden: hidden}
after
  ExPoser.Client.release([h])
end
```

Nothing is left behind:

```elixir
ExPoser.Client.stats()
```
